Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Zero 0.7.0 real-mode target

compiler.zero --real implements the real-mode u32 subset in Zero. It shares
Zero's data-first grammar and emits instructions directly. Its executable
metadata and architectural/BIOS ABI primitives do
not contain disk selection, retry, page-table or loader policy. Those
operations are expressed by bios.zero.

Build on Linux x86-64
Run bash build.sh to rebuild the Zero compiler and all system artifacts.
To compile an individual BIOS source with the resulting compiler:
./build/zero --real < bios.zero > build/bios.bin

The same executable provides the full-u64 x86-64/IA32 targets with their
explicit target options; see LANGUAGE.ztext and X86.ztext.

Language subset
Use SYNTAX.ztext's grammar: expression => @name. declarations, expression ->
@name. updates, @references, ? condition [ ] ~ [ ] branches, ?? condition [ ]
loops, :operation[arguments separated by |] values and !operation[arguments].
effects. Zero-argument effects use !halt., !enable. or !disable. Declarations
are top-level only; legacy syntax is not accepted.

Values are u32. Literals above 4294967295 reject; +, -, *, unary minus wrap
modulo 2^32. Comparisons are unsigned and produce 0 or 1. Strings are pointers
to embedded NUL-terminated ASCII. Arithmetic grouping uses parentheses.
Supported values include :load8/:load16/:load32, :in8/:in16, :reserve and
:arg0[], plus the hardware operations below. arg0 is preserved initial BIOS
DL, zero-extended. Smaller loads and port reads zero-extend.
:bitxor[left | right] and :bitand[left | right] operate on all32 value bits.
:shr[value | count] is logical right shift; counts>=32 return0.

Memory/port effects are !store8, !store16, !store32, !out8 and !out16 with
their documented argument brackets. load64/store64, arg1, call/call7, enter,
fork/switch and hosted put/exit/read/write/pread/pwrite/fsync are unavailable. Invalid or unsupported source
returns 1 with zero stdout; no foreign runtime is used. Check the exit status:
this compiler version does not yet print a source diagnostic.

Executable and storage ABI
Source<=262144 bytes; output<=16384 bytes;64 variables;31-byte names;
4095-byte string payloads; 64 block/expression levels. Output writes retry
EINTR and short writes. Host I/O errors can leave a partial output file.

Firmware loads the flat file at physical 7c00. Its512-byte executable header
contains a far jump to 0000:7e00, zero padding and 55AA at bytes510..511.
The header encodes entry metadata only, not a BIOS disk-reading policy.
The prologue establishes CS=0 via that header, DS=ES=SS=0, ESP=7c00,
EBP=7000, DF=0 and IF=0, and zeroes256 bytes of u32 local-variable slots.
Generated arithmetic uses 32-bit operands and required address overrides.
This is a 386-or-newer encoding target; hardware primitives and the BIOS
loader impose their own feature requirements. bios.zero requires CPUID.

Low-memory ownership before a transfer
4000..5fff    8192-byte reserve arena
6e00..6e03    preserved initial DL
6e04..6e07    aligned arena cursor
6e08..6e0b    temporary BIOS status
6e20..6e33    transfer arguments and far pointer
6f00..6fff    64 u32 variables
below 7c00     downward expression/firmware-call stack
7c00..bbff    maximum executable extent

The stack must not collide with reserved/local areas. :reserve[@size] rounds
up to 16-byte alignment, returns the old cursor and traps with UD2 on wrap or
exhaustion. It does not zero storage. Real-mode segment limits remain 64 KiB;
32-bit address arithmetic alone does not grant access above those limits.
Source must supply valid addresses and table contents.
The current BIOS loader deliberately never calls reserve: after disk reads
finish, it reuses 4000..5fff for two page directories. This is loader policy,
not an allocation available to arbitrary real-mode programs at the same time.

BIOS ABI operations
:bios[@drive | @dap]
  INT13 AH42, DL=drive, DS:SI=0000:dap. Source constructs the DAP and controls
  blocks, destination, LBA and retries. Returns0 when CF is clear; otherwise
  AH, or256 when CF is set with AH zero.
:biosreset[@drive]
  INT13 AH00, DL=drive; the same status conversion.
:bioscheck[@drive]
  INT13 AH41 with BX55AA. Returns0 only if CF is clear, BX becomesAA55 and
  CX bit 0 is set; otherwise 1.
!biosmode[@mode].
  INT10 AH00, AL=mode.
!biosput[@byte].
  INT10 AH0E, AL=byte, BH0, BL7.
:vbe[@ax | @bx | @cx | @buffer]
  INT10 with the supplied low AX/BX/CX and ES:DI=0000:buffer; returns AX.
  Buffer must fit 16 bits. The caller owns the full service-specific buffer
  and validates firmware output. Mode enumeration, formats, framebuffer
  mapping and mode-choice policy remain loader code.
:far16[@segment | @offset]
  Reads a zero-extended u16 through a real-mode segment. Segment must fit
  16 bits, offset must be <=65534, and the word must lie entirely below
  physical 0x100000. Invalid bounds trap with UD2. This permits reading a
  firmware far pointer without assuming DS=0 can address the entire first MiB.
:memmap[@continuation | @buffer]
  INT15/E820 with EAX=E820, EDX=SMAP, ECX=24 and ES:DI=0000:buffer.
  The buffer owns28 writable bytes at a physical address<=0xffe0. The wrapper
  initializes extended attributes at+20 to1 and stores returned ECX at+24.
  It returns the next EBX continuation (0 ends enumeration), or0xffffffff for
  an invalid buffer, CF, a wrong SMAP signature or returned size outside20..24.
  Invalid buffer is rejected before dereferencing it. The source distinguishes
  legacy20-byte records from extended24-byte records and validates usable RAM,
  overlaps and allocation policy. The compiler does not select memory regions.

Firmware wrappers preserve general registers and DS/ES, enable interrupts
for the firmware call, then restore incoming FLAGS/segments and clear DF.
Value results return separately in EAX. This is fixed firmware ABI encoding.
Use these operations only in real mode with a valid BIOS interrupt table.

Architectural operations
:cpuid[@leaf | @index] uses EAX=leaf/ECX=0. Index0/1/2/3 selects EAX/EBX/ECX/
EDX; any other index returns 0. The source must ensure CPUID exists.
:rdmsr[@index] returns the low 32 bits from RDMSR; the source must select a
supported MSR and account for high bits when relevant.
!lgdt[@address]. loads a six-byte GDTR with a 32-bit base.
!cr0[@value]. / !cr3[@value]. / !cr4[@value]. write the supplied register.
!wrmsr[@index | @low | @high]. writes the complete 64-bit MSR value.
!enable. / !disable. encode STI / CLI. !halt. enters a CLI/HLT loop.

!longjump[@selector | @entry | @arg | @stack].
  Source supplies GDT, valid mappings, CR3, CR4.PAE, EFER.LME and CR0.PE|PG
  first. The selected code descriptor has L=1/D=0. The primitive constructs
  its temporary m16:32 far pointer, reaches an inline 64-bit ABI shim, loads
  null DS/ES/SS, zero-extends entry/arg/stack to RAX/RCX/RSP, aligns RSP
  down to 16 bytes and jumps. It does not return.

!protectedjump[@selector | @entry | @arg | @stack].
  Source supplies a flat 32 code descriptor, flat writable data descriptor at
  selector 10h and CR0.PE with the required paging policy. The primitive far
  transfers to an inline 32-bit shim, loads DS/ES/SS/FS/GS=10h, clears DF,
  installs the aligned stack, and enters the supplied kernel with cdecl
  arg0 at [ESP+4], arg1=0 at [ESP+8], and a return-to-halt address at [ESP].
  Entry ESP modulo 16 is 12. The BIOS32 path leaves paging disabled and does
  not need PAE or long mode.

Neither transfer primitive constructs a GDT, page tables or memory map,
chooses a kernel, reads a disk, or implements CPU-feature policy.

Verification
Current-grammar real-mode programs booted in SeaBIOS/QEMU and produced exact
expected output. Arithmetic, wrapping bit extraction, comparisons, memory,
CPUID, saved DL and BIOS-wrapper behavior have dedicated regression coverage.
The BIOS dispatcher also uses protectedjump to enter the shared IA32 kernel.
Compiler rejection/boundary cases and final release boot evidence are
recorded in verification.ztext. VM success does not establish physical-hardware
compatibility or behavior for unsupported CPU/MSR combinations.
The0.7 standalone BIOS probe additionally passed90 u32 bit/shift cases, a full
E820 enumeration through continuation0, size/RAM checks, buffer/local canaries
and invalid-buffer error returns. Evidence: verification/compiler-fs/values/real-vm.


Dword port I/O
The386+ real-mode target supports :in32[port] and !out32[port | value]. with
operand-size prefixes. It uses the low16 port bits and its normalu32 value
width. PCI configuration policy remains source-owned. Native QEMU real-mode
PCI tests are recorded in verification/network-ports; see selfhost/port32.ztext.
